                        "form": "scringing"
                    },
                    {
                        "form_id": "544e8d38-fafb-4f5e-9a30-ff97a1e36ea8",
                        "entry_id": "9cec0b00-c379-3837-a462-24a5601a9c4c",
                        "tags": [
                            "participle",
                            "past"
                        ],
                        "form": "scringed"
                    },
                    {
                        "form_id": "6543fb7d-9bd0-4bf9-93b5-df98353d229f",
                        "entry_id": "9cec0b00-c379-3837-a462-24a5601a9c4c",
                        "tags": [
                            "past"
                        ],
                        "form": "scringed"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "e2d307f6-66a9-3e5f-b5ae-585f2bd17b88",
                        "entry_id": "9cec0b00-c379-3837-a462-24a5601a9c4c",
                        "glosses": [
                            "To cringe or shrink."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "UK",
                            "US",
                            "dialectal",
                            "intransitive"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "960bbc1e-b575-468a-9348-01e22dc13671",
                                "sense_id": "e2d307f6-66a9-3e5f-b5ae-585f2bd17b88",
                                "text": "It made me scringe to hear her, Sir, \" said she, adding, with a change of tone, \" but I must scringe for myself yet; for , oh dear! how can I talk about other people's sins, and I such a sinner!"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "c36feaaf-925a-4942-b11c-11ae1d21a556",
                                "sense_id": "e2d307f6-66a9-3e5f-b5ae-585f2bd17b88",
                                "text": "Yes, if you put your hand on him he might scringe around a little ."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "0dbb52aa-4639-43e2-abd6-ea60d936dbfd",
                                "sense_id": "e2d307f6-66a9-3e5f-b5ae-585f2bd17b88",
                                "text": "It allus made him scringe to have me speak of the Banty woman in the tone I used when I wanted to make him scringe, and I had a notion that flauntin ' his full name wouldn't soothe him much;"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "1b105629-9818-4909-bcfe-08449ecaa436",
                                "sense_id": "e2d307f6-66a9-3e5f-b5ae-585f2bd17b88",
                                "text": "But scringe from him, an' as Shakespeare says, clad in a little brief authority, that houn' dawg'll bite you when you ain't a-watchin' out."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "86b1c6db-8749-3938-a028-6d0df67c92fd",
                        "entry_id": "9cec0b00-c379-3837-a462-24a5601a9c4c",
                        "glosses": [
                            "To scrape."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "UK",
                            "US",
                            "dialectal",
                            "intransitive"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "c6c4900f-92ef-4001-8bd1-db88feb5aa87",
                                "sense_id": "86b1c6db-8749-3938-a028-6d0df67c92fd",
                                "text": "When a boy sharpens his slate-pencil with a knife, he says it makes his teeth 'scringe'."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "e8ce49e9-dd23-450e-8516-92088ee09d66",
                                "sense_id": "86b1c6db-8749-3938-a028-6d0df67c92fd",
                                "text": "Knee joints were beginning to scringe."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "33d2d27d-504d-4a2c-8f63-d4b137fcd819",
                                "sense_id": "86b1c6db-8749-3938-a028-6d0df67c92fd",
                                "text": "All her life Gert Kavanaugh had had to scringe and scrape to make ends meet while Marta May — a spoiled brat if Gert had ever seen one — had been the darling of the countryside."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "37d40e93-6448-3059-9025-eaacfe8edefc",
                        "entry_id": "9cec0b00-c379-3837-a462-24a5601a9c4c",
                        "glosses": [
                            "To fish using a scringe."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "83a06078-76ae-4aa6-aa51-b75b91454ba9",
                                "sense_id": "37d40e93-6448-3059-9025-eaacfe8edefc",
                                "text": "Trawling, or scringing, for salmon trout is, in some respects, similar to splashing, and is also carried on between the hours of sunset and sunrise, in the nooks, bays, and other suitable places along the sore of either the coast or of the sea-water loch, into which rivulets and streams empty themselves, although there are many places in which the splash net can be advantageously used where the scringe cannot be employed, as the latter requires a clear bottom, without any impediments, otherwise it cannot be drawn to the shore."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "fb9e8057-9bb9-40ee-ad67-3efe34463762",
                                "sense_id": "37d40e93-6448-3059-9025-eaacfe8edefc",
                                "text": "The evil complained of, so far from having decreased, has been greater than before, as the men engaged in this lawless pursuit, finding that they scringe with impunity, have become emboldened, and are more industrious than ever."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "2a2e45c3-4689-41f5-aeeb-82953bee9116",
                                "sense_id": "37d40e93-6448-3059-9025-eaacfe8edefc",
                                "text": "But in spite of their watchfulness, sometimes a black-looking boat slips over in the dead of night from somewhere in the neighbourhood of Oban, catches the keepers napping, and 'scringes' the mouth of a river, clearing off every salmon and sea-trout in the bay."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
